{"type":"event","event":{"type":"thinking_end","text":"The current implementation is O(n²) or worse (actually O(n³) with the set check). I need to implement the classic sliding window \"longest substring without repeating characters\" with tie-breaking preserving the earliest substring.\n\nKey detail: \"no repeated Unicode code points\". Python strings are sequences of Unicode code points, so `len(set(s))==len(s)` works on code points. A sliding window over the string works fine.\n\nTie-breaking: preserve earliest substring on ties. So if two windows have the same maximal length, we keep the one that starts earlier. The standard sliding window with \"if window length > best length, update\" naturally keeps the earliest, since we scan left to right — first time we reach the max length wins.\n\nWait, careful: with a sliding window, windows are enumerated in order of increasing right end. The earliest-start longest window... Let me think. For a fixed longest length L, the window that starts earliest also ends earliest (start + L - 1). As we slide the right pointer rightward, the first time we achieve length L is the window with the smallest end, hence smallest start among length-L windows? Not exactly — windows of length exactly L appear in order of start; the first window of length ≥ L encountered... Since we only update best when len > best_len (strictly greater), and the first window reaching length L is the one with the earliest start among all length-L windows (because windows of length L appear in start order as right advances), this is correct.\n\nActually, is the earliest-start window of maximum length always the first one to reach that length in the sliding-window scan? The sliding window enumerates windows as (left, right) pairs where for each right, left is the smallest index such that text[left:right+1] has no repeats. Every maximal window (left minimal for each right) is enumerated. Any window of length L (max) that has no repeats is a \"maximal window\" in the sense that its left is minimal for that right? If window text[a:a+L] has all distinct chars and L is the maximum, then extending left would give length > L which is impossible, so left is minimal for right = a+L-1. Yes. And windows are visited in increasing right order, so increasing start order for fixed length. Strict `>` update keeps the earliest. Good.\n\nBut one more subtlety: standard sliding window with \"shrink until no duplicate\" — using a dict of last occurrence, jump left = max(left, last[c]+1). The windows enumerated are (left_min_for_right, right). That enumerates all maximal windows. Good.\n\nAlternative: also there could be a subtlety about `len(set(s))==len(s)` on code points — Python 3 strings index by code point, so sliding window over the string handles Unicode code points naturally. Surrogate pairs aren't an issue in Python 3 (strings are code point sequences, unlike UTF-16).\n\nEdge cases: empty string → returns ''. Single char → that char.\n\nImplementation:\n\n```python\ndef longest_unique(text):\n    best_start = 0\n    best_len = 0\n    last = {}\n    left = 0\n    for right, ch in enumerate(text):\n        prev = last.get(ch, -1)\n        if prev >= left:\n            left = prev + 1\n        last[ch] = prev if False else right  # just last[ch] = right\n        cur_len = right - left + 1\n        if cur_len > best_len:\n            best_len = cur_len\n            best_start = left\n    return text[best_start:best_start + best_len]\n```\n\nSimplify: last[ch] = right.\n\nWait — tie-break check: first window achieving the max length. cur_len > best_len strict — first time length reaches L, best_start set; later windows of same length don't replace. And the first window to reach L has the smallest right among length-L windows... but does smallest right imply smallest start? For length-L windows, start = right - L + 1, so yes, smallest right = smallest start. Good.\n\nHmm, but one more check: could the earliest longest substring be missed? All maximal windows are enumerated exactly once each (for each right, the minimal valid left). Any longest substring is maximal (can't extend either way without... well, can't extend right since it's max length; can't extend left since then a longer window would exist). Actually can it extend left? If text[a-1:a+L] has no repeats, then it's a window of length L+1 > max — contradiction. So left is minimal. Yes, enumerated.\n\nComplexity: O(n) time, O(min(n, alphabet)) space. Handles hundreds of thousands of chars easily.\n\nLet me write it, test with the public test, plus additional tests including large input and tie cases and Unicode.\n\nAdditional verification: compare against brute force on random small strings, and timing on large input.\n\nLet me implement and run tests."}}
