no repeats is a \"maximal window\" in the sense that its left is minimal for that right? If window text[a:a+L] has all distinct chars and L is the maximum, then extending left would give length > L which is impossible, so left is minimal for right = a+L-1. Yes. And windows are visited in increasing right order, so increasing start order for fixed length. Strict `>` update keeps the earliest. Good.\n\nBut one more subtlety: standard sliding window with \"shrink until no duplicate\" — using a dict of last occurrence, jump left = max(left, last[c]+1). The windows enumerated are (left_min_for_right, right). That enumerates all maximal windows. Good.\n\nAlternative: also there could be a subtlety about `len(set(s))==len(s)` on code points — Python 3 strings index by code point, so sliding window over the string handles Unicode code points naturally. Surrogate pairs aren't an issue in Python 3 (strings are code point sequences, unlike UTF-16).\n\nEdge cases: empty string → returns ''. Single char → that char.\n\nImplementation:\n\n```python\ndef longest_unique(text):\n    best_start = 0\n    best_len = 0\n    last = {}\n    left = 0\n    for right, ch in enumerate(text):\n        prev = last.get(ch, -1)\n        if prev >= left:\n            left = prev + 1\n        last[ch] = prev if False else right  # just last[ch] = right\n        cur_len = right - left + 1\n        if cur_len > best_len:\n            best_len = cur_len\n            best_start = left\n    return text[best_start:best_start + best_len]\n```\n\nSimplify: last[ch] = right.\n\nWait — tie-break check: first window achieving the max length. cur_len > best_len strict — first time length reaches L, best_start set; later windows of same length don't replace. And the first window to reach L has the smallest right among length-L windows... but does smallest right imply smallest start? For length-L windows, start = right - L + 1, so yes, smallest right = smallest start. Good.\n\nHmm, but one more check: could the 